00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
it's been just over a week since apple's  m2 macbook pro has been out in the wild  and so far we've found seven problems  with it after one week some that aren't  as big of a deal and some that have  literally caused people to cancel their  orders which might actually be the right  move here because man apple kind of  messed up on the ssd situation but  before i get into that huge problem let  me first start off with some of the  other ones that we've noticed over the  past week the first problem is that the  m2 macbook pro uses exactly the same  design with absolutely no changes even  keeping the same touch bar which a lot  of people did not like including myself  meanwhile the macbook air gets a full  redesign with so many updates and  changes a beautiful display that's a  little bit larger with slimmer bezels  and basically an awesome new design but  with this one apple even reused old m1  macbook pro boxes and literally put a  new m2 sticker over the old one and  that's definitely not what apple is  known for doing this kind of shows that  apple doesn't really care about this  product or at least not enough to make  some new boxes with an updated wallpaper  to make people feel like it's a new  product now for problem number two  nothing else was upgraded in terms of  the actual feature set we have the same  exact speakers no changes same keyboard  same trackpad basically everything's the  same the display is the same no changes  at all it even has the same 720p webcam  while the less expensive m2 macbook air  is getting the 1080p webcam that is a  huge disappointment not only that but  this macbook is the only one that now is  not getting magsafe 3 for convenient and  fast charging now problem number three  has to do with apple's marketing which  is a bit overboard with this m2 macbook  pro first of all apple showed off that  the m2 chip supports 8k h.264 and hevc  encoders and decoders so this got me  very excited thinking that this m2  macbook pro is gonna be the best bang  for the buck video editing laptop out  there because it's equipped with the fan  and it has the m2 chip with 8k encoders  across the board however when we tested  a 5 minute 4k hevc export which is very  common it was only 5 seconds faster than  the m1 pro chip which has basically the  same encoders as the m1 now that is  hugely disappointing because apple never  marketed the m1 pro encoders as  supporting 8k but they did for the m2  and apparently 8k means 5 seconds faster  now it is nice that apple put in the pro  res media engine because it actually  does help quite a lot but the whole 8k  h.264 and hevc media engine part was way  overdoing it compared to the five second  difference that we actually experienced  now yes there might be some updates  coming but apple should honestly be  shipping the correct software updates  right away while launching new products  you shouldn't have to wait until mac os  ventura comes out or future updates to  use the machine at its full potential  and now moving on to problem number four  we have thermal throttling and there is  a lot to unpack here but i'm gonna start  with how much power the m2 chip uses in  the cinebench r23 stress test the m2  chip's cpu used 36 more power to get 13  more multi-core performance which is  definitely disappointing because it  shows that apple really had to boost the  clock speeds to squeeze out that extra  performance improvement and the issue  with overclocking the performance cores  that much is that it causes instability  which we noticed in our logic pro music  production stress test where the whole  point is to run as many tracks as  possible without overloading the chat  and the crazy thing is that the previous  m1 macbook pro was able to run 81 tracks  without overloading while the new m2  chip overloaded while running only 76  tracks which points to instability  within the chip and to prove that  stability is what matters for the logic  pro stress test intel's xeon chips  always performed much better in this  test compared to the regular core i9  cpus that were actually faster in terms  of raw cpu performance so stability  matters for this test but that's not the  end of the story because we experienced  severe thermal throttling on this m2  macbook pro more so than any macbook  we've ever used before what we did was  run an extreme stress test which is  exporting canon 8k raw from the canon r5  to 4k hevc now i will admit that this is  an extreme test that isn't meant for the  target market of the m2 macbook pro but  i specifically wanted to max out the cpu  and gpu as much as possible to stress  out the cooling system as much as  possible and honestly the truth is that  a computer's cooling system especially  one that's fully sealed like this empty  macbook pro and not upgradable should be  able to properly cool down the chip  that's inside of it without severely  thermal throttling because if it can't  that extra performance is being wasted  and the thing is that we ran this exact  extreme stress test on the previous m1  macbook pro and the gpu was stuck at 100  load the entire time without throttling  while requiring the fan to be basically  maxed out and then on the 14-inch  macbook pro it also adequately cooled  down the m1 pro chip without even maxing  out the fans but watch what happens to  the m2 macbook pro during the export  test the fan was stuck at maximum 7 200  rpms basically the entire time hitting  as much as 108 degrees celsius multiple  times which is higher than we've seen  even on intel max and since the fan was  already maxed out the system had no  choice but to drastically cut back on  the clock speeds with the performance  course going from 3200 megahertz to 1894  the efficiency core is going from 22 28  megahertz to 1444 and the gpu from 1393  to  289 megahertz all within a split second  which caused the temps to drop from 108  degrees to around 84 degrees celsius  before restarting the cycle over and  over again and the craziest thing was  that the m2 macbook pro cut the power to  the m2 chip from around 29.5 watts all  the way down to 7.3 watts which is the  most severe thermal throttling i've ever  seen on any macbook including intel  macbooks and when it finally finished  the test it was drastically slower than  the m1 pro macbook pro and it was only  10 faster than the previous m1 model  even though that model was gpu limited  the entire time which is disappointing  because without thermal throttling the  m2 cpu is 18 faster and the gpu is 35  faster so we should expect better  performance in this test and not only  just 10 and now moving on to problem  number five we have the ssd issue which  has basically blown up all over the  internet with the base model m2 macbook  pro with 256 gigs of ssd apple chose to  only fill one of the ssd spots on the  logic board compared to the previous m1  macbook pro which had both of the spots  filled and this is basically causing the  transfer speeds to be drastically  reduced as much as two times slower for  the read speed compared to the previous  m1 macbook pro and the crappy thing  about that is that it's gonna slow down  your large file transfers it could  potentially slow down the opening and  loading of apps and games compared to  the previous m1 macbook pro but what  really blew our mind was that this ssd  is slower than apple's ipad air 5 with  only a 64 gigabyte ssd and that ipad  starts at just six hundred dollars  instead of thirteen hundred dollars for  the m2 macbook pro and now for problem  number six not only did apple switch to  one nand ship instead of two on the base  model which instantly cuts away half of  the channels but the actual nand chips  themselves are worse quality than on  previous macbooks and to prove this we  actually tested the 512 gig ssd model of  the m2 macbook pro so what we did is  compare the m2 macbook pro to our  previous 16-inch intel 2019 macbook pro  model that seems like it has slower ssd  write speeds than the 512 gig ssd in the  m2 macbook pro according to the  blackmagic disc test and we did a large  file transfer and we found that it  finished faster on the old intel model  compared to this new m2 macbook pro with  a 512 gigabyte ssd and how is that  possible well it seems like apple is  starting to use lower quality ssds that  slow down more after a certain amount of  data is transferred specifically when  the intel 16 inches ssd slowed down it  continued to transfer at about 900  megabytes per second which is pretty  good but on the m2 macbook pro's 512 gig  ssd once it slowed down it went all the  way down to an average of around 350  megabytes per second which caused it to  transfer the file slower basically  almost a minute slower so what this  essentially means is that apple  downgraded the quality of these new nand  ssd chips compared to the previous ones  which means that apple is trying to save  money so we did a bit of research online  and we found that a  gig flash nand wafer costs apparently  only two dollars and four cents on the  chinese market which was updated today  as i'm filming this so this shows that  apple is trying to squeeze every ounce  of profit margin out of this m2 macbook  pro in order to keep the base price at  the same 1299 while upgrading it with  the m2 chip so from what it seems like  the supply chain and inflationary issues  are probably worse than we think with  apple making such a weird change going  from two chips to one on the base model  ruining the performance and in my  opinion apple made a huge mistake and  misjudgment by choosing to cut costs on  this specific part of the macbook pro  particularly because of the seventh and  final problem for this video the slow  256 gig base ssd is negatively impacting  the performance of the virtual memory  swap because when the very low eight  gigs of ram which honestly shouldn't  even be an option on the mt macbook pro  when that fills up it has to resort to  the ssd to use as virtual memory and the  slower the ssd is the slower that  virtual memory is as well which slows  down performance so we ended up testing  this by opening 10 chrome tabs in the  background and then running a couple of  real world tests and multiple times the  m2 macbook pro ended up being slower  than the previous m1 macbook pro this  should never happen the new model  performing worse than the old model this  is embarrassing and it's a huge fail by  apple for choosing to go with just that  one nand chip so with that said i can  safely say that apple messed up badly  with the m2 macbook pro and the worrying  part about all of this is that there's a  good chance that the m2 macbook air also  comes with the slow ssd issue as well as  likely suffering even more from thermal  throttling issues not only in extreme  tests like the one that we ran but  probably even in more of the common  tests as well so honestly i've got to  say that i'm a bit disappointed with the  m2 macbook pro so there you guys go  those were the top seven problems with  the m2 macbook pro and if you disagree  with any of them let me know down in the  comment section below but if you enjoyed  this video and it was insightful to you  go ahead and click the circle above to  subscribe because we have a lot more  testing to go especially the m2 macbook  air and definitely check out one of  those two right over there thanks for  watching and we'll see you in the next  video

